{
  "global_card_id": "strong_and_weak_highs_and_lows",
  "global_canonical": "Strong and Weak Highs and Lows",
  "type": "merged_concept",
  "school": "Price Action",
  "aliases": [
    "Strong Highs and Lows",
    "Weak Highs and Lows",
    "Strong and Weak Levels",
    "Strong and Weak Price Levels"
  ],
  "definition": "Strong highs and lows are swing points whose subsequent move breaks prior market structure (BOS, or CHoCH per ICT), confirming decisive momentum and trend continuation or reversal. Weak highs and lows arise from moves that fail to break the preceding structure, signaling indecision, exhaustion, or potential consolidation. Strong levels act as reliable support/resistance zones; weak levels are prone to being broken and often precede reversals.",
  "definition_per_source": {
    "SMC-Strong-Weak-Supplement (strong_and_weak_highs_and_lows)": "Strong highs/lows are swing points from which price breaks prior market structure (BOS), confirming trend continuation or reversal. Weak highs/lows fail to break the preceding swing high or low, indicating a lack of momentum and potential consolidation or trend failure.",
    "SMC-Strong-Weak-Supplement (strong_and_weak_levels)": "Strong levels originate from moves that break market structure (BoS) or change character (CHoCH), indicating decisive momentum and acting as key support/resistance. Weak levels come from moves that fail to break structure, suggesting indecision and being less reliable.",
    "SMC-Strong-Weak-Supplement (strong_and_weak_price_levels)": "Strong levels originate from moves that break market structure, indicating institutional conviction. Weak levels come from moves that fail to break structure, signaling a lack of momentum and potential reversal."
  },
  "identification_rules": [
    "A low is strong if the subsequent upward move breaks the previous swing high (BOS in an uptrend); it is weak if that move fails to break the previous swing high and creates no higher high.",
    "A high is strong if the subsequent downward move breaks the previous swing low (BOS in a downtrend); it is weak if that move fails to break the previous swing low.",
    "To locate a strong level, find a break of structure (new high in an uptrend / new low in a downtrend) and trace back to the origin of the move that caused the break — that starting point is the strong level.",
    "To locate a weak level, find a move that attempted but failed to break structure; the origin of that failed move is the weak level.",
    "ICT sources note that BOTH Break of Structure (BoS) and Change of Character (CHoCH) moves produce strong levels; only moves that neither break structure nor change character yield weak levels."
  ],
  "common_mistakes": [
    "Assuming a level is strong simply because it is a swing high or low, without checking whether the subsequent move actually broke structure.",
    "Misidentifying a weak level as strong because it visually appears as support/resistance, without analyzing the structural context."
  ],
  "trading_implication": "Strong highs/lows mark high-probability support/resistance zones backed by structural breaks (and, per ICT, institutional conviction); traders use them as confirmation of trend strength, often entering on pullbacks to these levels and setting stops and targets around them since they are more likely to hold and produce reactions. Weak highs/lows signal exhaustion, false attempts, or range-bound conditions; traders treat them with caution, anticipating break failures or reversals, and use them to avoid false breakouts or to exit. Distinguishing the two helps filter trade setups and anticipate structural shifts.",
  "merge_notes": "Sources are conceptually aligned. Two cards are tagged ICT and one Price Action, but the underlying logic (structural break = strong, failed break = weak) is shared. Key cross-source nuances: the ICT cards explicitly extend strong-level qualification to CHoCH moves (not just BoS) and frame strong levels as evidence of institutional conviction, whereas the Price Action card frames them purely in terms of BOS and trend momentum.",
  "source_cards": [
